  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe latest radar and satellite imagery depict a closed upper-\nlevel low slowly pivoting northeast across the Ohio Valley this\nmorning, with periods of light to moderate rain impacting the \narea. Rain will gradually begin to weaken in intensity by this\nafternoon and evening as the upper-level low lifts northeast\ninto Canada, though residual light rain showers and/or drizzle\nwill remain possible as weak troughing persists across the Lower\nGreat Lakes. The most widespread and steady rain is expected\nthis morning as the surface low passes directly overhead. \nAdditional rainfall amounts through tonight will range between \none and three-quarters of an inch, with the higher amounts \ngenerally found along the lakeshore or east of the I-71 \ncorridor. Wind chills are expected to remain in the 30s or\nperhaps even upper 20s at times today as winds shift towards the\nwest to northwest, remaining elevated with gusts of 20 to 30\nmph. Rain showers will taper by Saturday morning as weak high \npressure briefly builds across the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA sharp upper-level trough will swing east through the Great\nLakes on Sunday into Monday, bringing the next chance for\nwidespread precipitation. At the surface, a compact low is\nexpected to develop and move east across the Lower Great Lakes,\nextending a cold front into the area and briefly ushering in a\ncolder air mass on Monday. Will need to monitor trends in the \nwind field with this system as strong cold air advection could \nresult in some gustier winds (35 to 45 mph) translating down to \nthe surface, especially Sunday night into Monday morning. \nRainfall amount will generally be a half inch or less and may \nalso need to watch the potential for some wet snowflakes to mix \nin as the colder air mass arrives by Monday morning.",
